               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="138X/139X" notBefore="1380" notAfter="1399"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xiv ex.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#l"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_558">Tynemouth, Northumberland.
                        Benedictine priory of St Mary the Virgin and St Oswin; cell of St
                        Albans</orgName><p>This manuscript and Fitzwilliam Museum 1-1973 once formed
                        one volume. The latter (q.v.) shows special devotion to St Oswin, and so the
                        two parts comes from Tynemouth, a cell of St Albans.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_474">St Albans,
                        Hertfordshire, Benedictine abbey of St Alban</orgName>
                     <p>The status of this attribution has changed over time. It was first included
                        in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), at
                        which point the designation was accepted. The corresponding Medieval
                        Libraries of Great Britain index card reflects the 1964 status, and provides
                        the following evidence: <q>In litany the list of martyrs, 39 in all, begins
                           with Alban, Amphibalus cum sociis, and Oswin. The suffrages include Alban
                              <q>extra monasterium et in cellis</q>, Amphibalusm and Oswin.</q>
                        However, this attribution was later rejected in the Supplement (1987) in
                        favour of Tynemouth (see above). For evidence that this is Tynemouth rather
                        than St Albans, see the Fitzwilliam Museum Syndicate's Annual Report (1973),
                        p. 12.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q><persName>Edmund Dudley</persName>
                        1504</q> (erased, fol. 33).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">There is also an erased inscription at the
                     top of fol. 33v, with 'Beaumont' at the foot.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Sotheby's, sale of 26 February-3
                     March 1900, lot 1190.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Bought by the Fitzwilliam in 1910 for £30
                     from <persName role="fmo">H. T. Wake</persName>. Fritchley, Derby.</provenance>
               </history>
